      "feedback": "Yes, \"Reed Palm\" and \"Bamboo Palm\" are two common names for the exact same species (Chamaedorea seifrizii). In fact, \"Bamboo Palm\" is already listed right under your Also Known As section at the bottom of the page, so you're totally solid there.\n\nLooking at this draft for the Reed Palm, it has great raw material. Applying the exact same discipline we used on the Fiji Fan Palm—stripping out editorialized drama, removing textbook fluff, and keeping facts grounded—here is how to tighten it up.\n\nWhat Left Me Confused, Missing, or Misleading\n\nMissing—The Needle Science (Calcium Oxalate): You note in Edibility & Toxicity that the ripe berries irritate skin. Don't just leave it at \"can irritate skin\"—explain why. The fruit flesh is packed with microscopic calcium oxalate crystals (raphides). If a visitor squishes a black berry, those tiny microscopic needles puncture the skin. That turns a generic caution into memorable plant physics.\n\nMissing Historical Context (Berlin Specimen): Bullet #2 i",
